<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="49505" href="schedule/2015/spring/PSYC/593/49505.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2015" href="schedule/2015.xml">2015</calendarYear><term id="120151" href="schedule/2015/spring.xml">Spring 2015</term><subject id="PSYC" href="schedule/2015/spring/PSYC.xml">Psychology</subject><course id="593" href="schedule/2015/spring/PSYC/593.xml">Seminar</course></parents><sectionNumber>AL2</sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Classics in Psychology</sectionTitle><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>"Ever wonder why we are here? By "here", I mean the current state of theories in Psychology. To understand this *why*, it is good to know the wheres and whens: where and when did these ideas come from? What have they replaced or improved upon? In this seminar, we will trace back Psychological Theory over the last 140 years or so, by reading and discussing classic articles and opinion pieces written by the movers and shakers of our discipline since its modern inception."</sectionText><sectionNotes>Restricted to Graduate - Urbana-Champaign.</sectionNotes><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>Open (Restricted)</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2015-01-20Z</startDate><endDate>2015-05-06Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>01:30 PM</start><end>03:20 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>F      </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>27</roomNumber><buildingName>Psychology Building</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Lleras" firstName="A">Lleras, A</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>